                <head><hi rendition="#small-caps">Annex I</hi></head>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#italic">Telegram Despatched on March 26th to
                        Germans Through the International Armistice Commission, Spa, From the
                        Supreme Economic Council</hi></p>
                <p>With reference to communications already addressed to the German Government
                    regarding the import of foodstuffs and the export of commodities in pursuance of
                    the Brussels Agreement, the following supplementary announcement is made: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>That with a view to assisting Germany to obtain credits in Neutral
                            Countries for the purchase of food supplies in accordance with the
                            promise of consideration given by the Delegates at Brussels, the
                            Associated Governments state that no firm in any Neutral Country will in
                            the future incur the penalty of blacklisting because it has: <list>
                                <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)</label>
                                <item>exported foodstuffs to Germany within the limits approved by
                                    and in accordance with the regulations of the Associated
                                    Governments;</item>
                                <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>)</label>
                                <item>afforded credits to Germany for the purchase of such
                                    foodstuffs;</item>
                                <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>)</label>
                                <item>imported goods permitted by the Associated Governments to be
                                    exported from Germany.</item>
                            </list></p></quote></p>
                <p>Negotiations and trading by Germany with firms in Neutral Countries, even though
                    on the Black Lists, will be permitted, subject to the approval of the Superior
                    Blockade Council.</p>
            </div>
